{"data":{"id":"10.17632/tnzghkdgfw.1","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.17632/tnzghkdgfw.1","prefix":"10.17632","suffix":"tnzghkdgfw.1","identifiers":[],"alternateIdentifiers":[],"creators":[{"name":"Hoa, Nguyen","nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nguyen","familyName":"Hoa","affiliation":["Hanoi National University of Education"],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"name":"Le, Van Khoa","nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Van Khoa","familyName":"Le","affiliation":["Hanoi National University of Education"],"nameIdentifiers":[{"schemeUri":"https://orcid.org","nameIdentifier":"https://orcid.org/0009-0007-4386-9872","nameIdentifierScheme":"ORCID"}]}],"titles":[{"title":"Step-level evidence integration in bayesian knowledge tracing: A controlled within-model ablation study"}],"publisher":"Mendeley Data","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence"},{"subject":"Education"},{"subject":"Applied Computer Science"}],"contributors":[{"name":"Hanoi National University of Education","nameType":"Organizational","contributorType":"Other","nameIdentifiers":[{"schemeUri":"https://ror.org","nameIdentifier":"https://ror.org/0360g3z42","nameIdentifierScheme":"ROR"}],"affiliation":[]}],"dates":[{"date":"2026-09-22T16:51:10Z","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"ris":"DATA","bibtex":"misc","citeproc":"dataset","schemaOrg":"Dataset","resourceType":"Dataset","resourceTypeGeneral":"Dataset"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.17632/tnzghkdgfw","resourceTypeGeneral":"Dataset","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":null,"rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"info:eu-repo/semantics/openAccess"},{"rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial 4.0 International","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-4.0","rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX"}],"descriptions":[{"description":"This dataset and notebook support a study of whether intermediate step-level evidence provides additional predictive information in Bayesian Knowledge Tracing (BKT) beyond a single terminal correctness outcome. The central hypothesis is that collapsing a multi-step learning episode into one binary response discards meaningful information about the learner’s problem-solving process, and that integrating the observed scaffold-step responses can improve discrimination of subsequent performance.\n\nThe analysis uses the corrected ASSISTments 2009–2010 Skill Builder dataset. A strict evaluation cohort is constructed from scaffolded episodes in which: (1) exactly one original problem interaction is observed; (2) that original response is incorrect; (3) all scaffold interactions occur after the original response; (4) the original problem is associated with exactly one non-null skill; (5) the learner completes the full scaffold sequence authored for the assistment; and (6) an immediate subsequent original opportunity on the same skill is observed. This procedure yields 8,995 evaluation instances from 1,494 learners across 33 skills.\n\nFor each episode, step-level evidence is represented as the ordered binary correctness vector of the scaffold responses. The prediction target is correctness on the learner’s immediate next original opportunity on the same skill.\n\nThree matched BKT conditions are compared: Terminal-BKT, which represents the current episode only by the original failure; Sequential Step-BKT, which processes the same scaffold responses as sequential observations without learning transitions between scaffold steps; and Length-normalized Step-BKT, which integrates the same responses using the mean log-likelihood ratio and applies one learning transition after the episode. All conditions share the same fitted BKT parameters, pre-episode mastery state, learners, evaluation instances, and prediction targets.\n\nThe primary findings show pooled out-of-fold AUCs of 0.7116, 0.7202, and 0.7382 for Terminal-BKT, Sequential Step-BKT, and Length-normalized Step-BKT, respectively. The length-normalized condition improves AUC over Terminal-BKT by 0.0266 and over the sequential control by 0.0180. Learner-cluster bootstrap confidence intervals are used for primary inference. RMSE and Brier-score results do not indicate a corresponding calibration improvement; therefore, the main finding should be interpreted as improved discrimination rather than improved calibration.\n\nThe repository is intended to support reproducibility of this narrow empirical claim. It does not establish superiority over deep knowledge-tracing architectures, general multi-path DAG inference, or generalization beyond scaffold-completed failure episodes.","descriptionType":"Abstract"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"xml":"","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/tnzghkdgfw/1","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"api","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"viewsOverTime":[],"downloadCount":0,"downloadsOverTime":[],"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"citationsOverTime":[],"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-09-22T16:51:12.000Z","registered":"2026-09-22T16:51:12.000Z","published":"2026","updated":"2026-09-22T16:51:12.000Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"bl.mendeley","type":"clients"}},"provider":{"data":{"id":"elsevier","type":"providers"}},"media":{"data":{"id":"10.17632/tnzghkdgfw.1","type":"media"}},"references":{"data":[]},"citations":{"data":[]},"parts":{"data":[]},"partOf":{"data":[]},"versions":{"data":[]},"versionOf":{"data":[{"id":"10.17632/tnzghkdgfw","type":"dois"}]}}}}